---
/*
  Terms.

  Written to match what the site actually does and what the submit page already promises,
  because the two consent checkboxes on /submit/ are the real agreement and this page only
  spells them out. If they ever disagree, the checkbox wins: it is the thing somebody
  ticked. The wording to keep in step is in src/pages/submit.astro around the
  consent_scrubbed and consent_publish inputs.

  Not written by a lawyer. See the note at the foot, which is deliberately on the page
  rather than only in a comment.
*/
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config/site';

const lastChanged = new Date(SITE.termsUpdated).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', {
  day: 'numeric',
  month: 'short',
  year: 'numeric'
});
---

<Base
  title="Terms"
  description="What you agree to by using the site, what happens to a story you send in, and who owns what."
>
  <main id="main">
    <div class="shell read">
      <aside class="rail">
        <dl>
          <div><dt>Who runs this</dt><dd><a href="/about/">Michael Jolley</a></dd></div>
          <div><dt>Your words</dt><dd>Stay yours</dd></div>
          <div><dt>The rules</dt><dd><a href="/conduct/">Code of conduct</a></dd></div>
          <div><dt>Last changed</dt><dd>{lastChanged}</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </aside>

      <article data-pagefind-body data-pagefind-filter="kind:page">
        <header class="storyhead">
          <span class="path">terms</span>
          <h1>
            Your words stay yours.
            <span class="squiggle"><span>Everything else is detail.</span></span>
          </h1>
          <p class="lede">
            This is a personal site run by one person. There is no company behind it, no
            support desk, and no small print designed to catch you out. What follows is the
            arrangement, written the way I would explain it out loud.
          </p>
        </header>

        <div class="prose terms-body">
          <div class="callout">
            <span class="tag">The short version</span>
            <p>
              You keep the copyright in anything you write here. You give me permission to
              publish it. You can ask for it to come down and I will take it down. Be decent,
              which is covered properly in the <a href="/conduct/">code of conduct</a>.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h2>Who owns what</h2>
          <p>
            The articles, videos, and dev disaster write ups that I produce are mine. Read
            them, quote them, link them, argue with them. If you want to republish something
            whole, ask, and the answer is usually yes.
          </p>
          <p>
            Stories you send in, and comments left before that feature was retired, are yours.
            You keep the copyright. What you give me is permission, not ownership: permission
            to publish it on this site, to edit it for length and clarity, to talk about it in
            a video, and to keep it up while it is still relevant. That permission is not
            exclusive, so the same story is still yours to tell anywhere else, including
            somewhere that pays you for it.
          </p>
          <p>
            The one thing I will not do is change what a story means. Trimming a paragraph is
            editing. Changing the outcome, the cause, or how it felt is not, and if a story
            needs that much work I will ask you rather than do it.
          </p>

          <h2>Taking something down</h2>
          <p>
            Email me and it comes down. You do not need a reason, and I am not going to
            negotiate about it. This is in the consent you tick when you send a story in,
            and it is the term I would least want somebody to discover was missing.
          </p>
          <p>
            Older comments are no longer shown on the site. Deleting your account turns them
            into tombstones so the stored discussion history stays consistent.
          </p>

          <h2>What you promise about a story</h2>
          <p>
            Two things, and you tick both before it sends. That it happened to you or you
            were there for it, and that you have taken out anything identifying your
            employer, your coworkers, or a customer.
          </p>
          <p>
            That second one matters more than it looks. This site exists because people can
            admit to breaking things without it costing them, and the fastest way to end that
            is one story where somebody works out which outage it was. I check every
            submission for it as well, but I only know what I can spot from outside.
          </p>
          <p>
            Do not send me something covered by an agreement you signed. I have no way of
            knowing, and it is your name on that agreement rather than mine.
          </p>

          <h2>What happens to a story after you send it</h2>
          <p>
            It goes into a queue only I can see. A title and a web address get drafted for it
            automatically, then I read it, fix the title if the machine got it wrong, check it
            for anything identifying, and file it under a severity. Nothing publishes without
            me reading it first.
          </p>
          <p>
            If story notifications are on, the site emails you when the story publishes and
            if it reaches the front page. A story I do not publish does not send a rejection
            email, so your account remains the complete record of what you sent and where it
            stands. Publishing anonymously keeps your name and handle off the page and off
            the source. The badge and notification still follow the account rather than the
            byline.
          </p>
          <p>
            I do not have to publish anything, and not publishing a story is not a judgement
            about you or about the story. Most of the time it is that I already have three
            that are too similar.
          </p>

          <h2>Your account</h2>
          <p>
            Sign in with an auth provider, pick a handle, and it is yours until you delete it or I close
            it. I close accounts for the things listed in the <a href="/conduct/">code of
            conduct</a> and for nothing else. Handles that impersonate somebody, or that are
            reserved for parts of the site, are not available.
          </p>
          <p>
            Deleting your account is a button on <a href="/account/">your account page</a>
            and it works immediately. What survives it is set out in the
            <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a>, and you should read that part before you
            press it rather than after.
          </p>

          <h2>Where this site sends you</h2>
          <p>
            Links out are links out. Once you are on YouTube, Twitch, GitHub or anywhere else,
            their terms apply and mine have stopped. Links people put on their profiles carry
            <code>nofollow</code> and are not an endorsement from me.
          </p>

          <h2>The part every site has</h2>
          <p>
            The site is provided as it is. I run it carefully, but I am one person, and it can
            be down, wrong, or briefly very strange. Everything published here is somebody's
            experience rather than professional advice, and a dev disaster is a story about
            what went wrong rather than instructions for your production database. Read it,
            learn from it, and make your own call.
          </p>
          <p>
            If something here causes you a loss, I am sorry, and I cannot be liable for it,
            to the extent the law where you live lets me say that. Where it does not, the
            law wins.
          </p>

          <h2>Changing these terms</h2>
          <p>
            The date is in the sidebar and it only moves when something actually changed. A
            change never applies backwards to a story you already sent under the old wording.
          </p>

          <div class="callout">
            <span class="tag">Not a lawyer</span>
            <p>
              I wrote this myself, in plain English, because a page nobody can read protects
              nobody. It has not been through a solicitor yet. If you are reading it as a
              lawyer and something here is unenforceable or missing, tell me and I will fix
              it rather than defend it.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="hero-actions terms-acts">
          <a class="btn primary" href="/submit/">Tell your story</a>
          <a class="btn" href="/privacy/">Privacy</a>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .terms-body {
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }
  .terms-acts {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
  }
</style>
